From c9b21948d80efb029182a80d7927ec70566f932b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 17:46:51 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] cuda/hip: multi-column waves for the prefill gated_delta_net
 (+5.7% prefill)

At pp512 gated_delta_net is 11.5% of the pass: 30 launches of 442 us, the
single largest prefill item left after 0040. The kernel is not memory bound and
it is not launch bound. It launches 4096 waves over 128 SIMDs, 32 waves per
SIMD, and spends 442 us on 512 serial tokens - 65 cycles per token per wave
slot against roughly 39 VALU ops of actual work. It is issue bound, and half of
those 39 ops are overhead.

The overhead is the reduction. The stock mapping gives one column of the
recurrent state to a whole wave: 32 lanes hold 4 rows each, and each token needs
two row-sharded dot products (S^T k and S^T q) turned into scalars, so two
5-step DPP butterflies - 20 VALU ops - pay for 12 ops of arithmetic.

gated_delta_net_mc_cuda gives CPW columns to one wave instead. The wave splits
into CPW groups of 32/CPW lanes; each group owns one column and a proportionally
taller contiguous row shard. Per column the arithmetic is untouched - the same
four-row fma chains over the same rows - but the butterfly shrinks to
log2(32/CPW) steps, because the levels it drops are exactly the levels the lane
can now do in registers, where they replace adds it was already doing. At CPW=4
that is 19 ops per column against 39, and the measured kernel goes
442 -> 283 us, 1.56x.

The stock kernel is left verbatim and still serves the decode shape. The
multi-column kernel is gated on n_tokens > 1 and on the vec4 layout, so at
decode nothing changes: same kernel, same geometry, same registers, and no new
branch on the path untouched call sites run (0040).

Measured (runner box, HIP_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0, whole-process interleaved A/B
against a control binary built from the parent commit, rotated order, 5 rounds):

  arm    pp512 med   vs control     tg128 med   vs control
  ctl      4376.98      -            148.54       -
  CPW=1    4367.91    1.0005         148.34     0.9984
  CPW=2    4574.74    1.0489         148.43     0.9987
  CPW=4    4615.17    1.0571         148.41     0.9991
  CPW=8    4605.63    1.0522         148.36     0.9988

CPW=4 ships. pp arms disjoint 5/5 (min 4601.28 above max control 4447.88). tg
arms overlap the control in all four cases - the decode path is not touched.
CPW=1 at 1.0005 is the evidence that the added kernels cost the untouched path
nothing.

Not bit-exact, and the reason is worth recording: this tree is built with
-funsafe-math-optimizations, so LLVM reassociates the accumulation in each
kernel however it likes and two differently shaped sources will not agree no
matter how carefully the source-level reduction order is matched. The difference
is ULP-scale and was classified by dumping the outputs of the op for the first
prefill launch of every layer, CPW=1 vs CPW=4: attn max 9 ULP, max relative
6.7e-7; state max relative 3.5e-5 on values of order 1e-9. CPW=1 reproduces
itself byte-for-byte across two loads, so the op is deterministic and this is
purely the reassociation.

- gate-shape ppl -c 512 --chunks 8 (the fold DOES fire here): see README, in
  band and inside the load-to-load spread of the control
- decode-path ppl -b 512 -ub 1 --chunks 8: 3.9338, identical to the banked
  stack - the multi-column kernel is gated off the decode shape by construction
- firing proven by kernel-census diff: 60 launches of
  gated_delta_net_mc_cuda<128,false,false,4> replace 60 of
  gated_delta_net_cuda<128,false,false,true> at the prefill shape
- test-backend-ops GATED_DELTA_NET / SSM_CONV OK
- long context tg neutral: d16384 136.95 -> 136.66, d32768 126.90 -> 126.72
- llama-server greedy 2/6 byte-identical, the rest diverging after 101-408 B,
  all completions non-empty and at least 276 B - the expected consequence of a
  float32 regrouping in front of a top-8 router
- GGML_GDN_COLS_PER_WAVE=1|2|4|8 re-sweeps it; 1 restores the stock kernel

